Need to implement a pipeline so that if the user ever calls a function loadAudio(std::string or char* fileLocation),
it will lazily load the first time the engine sees a call to that exact URL. once resolved, this will send to a consumer
on a background thread and move on returning an pending WaviateAudio object. If pending or loading still, the function will essentially return an audio clip of length 1 sample and value 0 in that sample.
once loaded, the WaviateAudio object will let users sample it in a few ways. clip.read(float x)
this acts exactly like texture sampling but its 1d, and it has optional args. one enum arg which sets the sample end behavior. clamp to 01, wrap, or reflect (zigzag/triangle wave off it).
another that lets the user choose nearest neighbor, linear interp (default), cubic, etc
you can also do clip.readSample(sampleIndex) for direct lookups of exact samples.
clip.length() is a function as well. Internally a WaviateAudio object is actually a wrapper style class. it should not hold a lot of data, but rather reference a position in a cache of loaded audios.
When an audio is loaded, the url becomes the key to a hashmap or some fast structure. the value becomes all the internal data. the WaviateAudio object returned either has a nullptr in which case its interface
acts as if it is length 1 with a single sample of 0 or it returns with a pointer to the cached audio data that URL is connected to. That way we can have loadAudio called per sample safely because its barely an allocation
per shader call. 

As part of this, implement the main pipeline to set this up. the classes and enums it needs to function, the placement of this as a function in the code and so on. Just don't do the file loading IO as part of this change yet.
